Ladybird limits document.write() and document.writeln() to 21 nested
calls per document. Once a write reaches that limit, further writes
are ignored until the outermost write returns. Independent writes
can then proceed, and other documents retain their own limits.

Blink enforces the same limit in Document::write(), with the depth in
write_recursion_depth_ and suppression in write_recursion_is_too_deep_.
WebKit uses Document::write() with m_writeRecursionDepth for the depth
and m_writeRecursionIsTooDeep to suppress further writes. Both engines
silently discard writes once the limit is reached. Ladybird uses 21
to match their observable limit of 22 script elements when a script
writes its own outerHTML during the initial document parse.

Gecko also permits 21 nested writes in Document::WriteCommon(): It
checks mWriteLevel against NS_MAX_DOCUMENT_WRITE_DEPTH (20) before
incrementing it. Its mTooDeepWriteRecursion suppresses writes until
the outermost call returns. Gecko throws NS_ERROR_UNEXPECTED when
the limit is reached; Ladybird silently ignores the write, matching
Blink and WebKit. This bounds recursive parser entry beyond the
HTML document write steps, which specify no recursion limit.
